Pete Ley wrote:
>
> Ach my dog just wanders around aimlessly

Seems to.  There are biases that you are not noticing.  Pets move
towards certain stuff a fraction of the time and move randomly a
fraction of the time.  That's enough that if you know what they move
towards you can steer them effectively but slowly.

Pets do not walk into walls, boulders or monsters including you.  This
sounds obvious but a pet in a horizontal or vertical has to stay ahead
or you or behind you.  A pet in a diagonal corridor can pass you when
you are in one of the corner squares but not when you are in one in the
between squares.  A pet near a wall can be steered somewhat by moving to
reduce the number of squares it can move to next.

Incidentally I dance and manuever the watch into rooms doing that type
of dancing.  I can't push them into a square without starting a fight,
but I can stand in their way and keep doing that until they end up where
I want them to be.  A monster in the open as 8 choices to move.  If I
stand next to it there are 7 choices.  Moving is 1/14th as fast as a
direct walk but it works.  A monster near a wall has 5 choices.  If I
stand next to it there are 4 choices.  Moving 1/8th as fast as a direct
walk still works.  If a monster is at a doorway and I am next to the
monster there is are 2-3 options including moving into the door.  If at
first you don't succeed try, try again.  The dance works far better with
pets than with peaceful monsters.

Pets avoid stepping on cursed objects.  When dropping a stack put a
cursed item on the top of the pile to channel your pet's movements.

Pets rush towards desired food items they can see.  It there's a tripe
ration in view you need to rush to it and pick it up or your pet will
get their first.  If the pet is hungry enough it will go for other types
of food.

Pets move towards monsters they can see whether you can see the monster
of not.  It uses the pet's line of sight.  Pets stay to fight monsters
they ahve decided to fight.  Pets retreat from combat with peacefuls
once they are down to half of their hit points.

Pets move towards you or they move in the last direction they saw you
move.  So if you go down a corridor and the pet does not follow try
again.

If you carry a tripe ration they move towards that as well as move
towards you.  It gives a higher chance of staying close to you.  This
effect is strong enough that a pet might not explore an entire big shop
if you stay next to the door.

> and displacing him seems to just put him in a random location. 

Displacing your pet swaps your positions.  That's not random.

The deal is - How much attention do you want to pay to your pet?  How
much extra time do you want to spend to keep your pet with you?  The
more you know about pet movements the less the effort is.  Having the
right items makes it very easy.  To me it's worth keeping my pet until I
return from mines end and let my pet wipe out the watch for their stuff.

The later in the game I am the less I care about my original pet.  Pets
are great for robbing shops and detecting cursed items, but after some
point they are a trade off of running interference for you at the price
of the effort of steering them.  A lease and a magic whistle makes a
very big difference.  But as I descend I often stop caring if my
original pet survives some level once I am in the mid-game past the
mines and Sokoban.

Very late in the game I might be able to tame pets in droves.  I can
wade through a crowd of pets or fight my way through a crowd of
hostiles.  If the crowd of hostiles is balrogs and archons it's not an
easy fight.  I'd rather Death fight with one of a dozen pets to get to
me than fight with me.


